so around a year ago we started working  on hazel's animation system this was  something that existed to some extent  already pretty early on in the engine it  was a very simple and primitive system  back then some of you will remember that  i made a devlog about it a while ago  what you're watching at the moment is a  scene that tim made using  hazel's brand new animation system which  was primarily developed by xerox you may  remember him from such videos as  testing hazel's performance yeah he's  been around for quite a while now what i  thought i would show you guys today is a  little bit of what the development  process of this animation system was  actually like  it all started with this message from  xerox for animation going forward will  you be rolling your own or plan to  integrate something like oz  which is an open source c plus skeletal  animation library and toolset as you can  see to which i replied well i was  planning to roll my own but this looks  interesting i'll check it out now  me being me when i say thanks i'll check  it out i probably mean something more  along the lines of i will someday in my  life probably take a look at this thing  so about a month later  this xerox had just gone ahead and  slapped it in there were some issues  like for example the dependency on the  fbx sdk was something i was not too  thrilled about but a few days later it  was basically working although i was  told to not get too excited always a  good idea to profile your work and see  how it performs and in this case  well it ended up being pretty good now  because we're building a game engine  here it's very very important to  consider the workflow how is this  actually going to work and integrate  with the existing asset system how are  we going to be able to author animations  and just in general deal with them in  the editor in an actual production  quality way i think that's something  that a lot of people overlook  that kind of whole workflow system and  architecture  in a lot of ways is actually much more  challenging to design and implement  compared to the actual like physical  animation code as usual there ended up  being a bunch of issues that xerox and i  had to work through and who could forget  the physics system another super  important thing when you're working on a  game engine there are various systems  they have to work together not  everything is just an isolated test that  makes things more difficult and time  consuming and hey i mean it can be a  struggle sometimes lots of debugging  later and some excitement it was finally  starting to come together workflow and  all good things always take time we also  discovered that asim the library we were  using for like 3d model loading had its  problems  as i said it's a long process it was  around this time that we decided to kind  of officially switch to gltf as the most  supported hazel 3d model format now all  of this work had got us to a pretty good  position we were able to import  animations apply them to various meshes  we could switch between various  animation states as you're seeing in  this scene right here of course it would  be nice to blend between animation  states but hey has to start somewhere so  overall pretty good progress and then a  few months later out of nowhere xerox  had decided to rewrite the animation  system this time without using oz  animation but instead replacing it with  our own custom system we even briefly  considered using this new animation  system in lotum there 50 but ultimately  decided against it looks like xerox had  fulfilled the prophecy the new system  was working really well and the  performance was great too we finally had  the beginnings of a solid animation  workflow in hazel i say the beginning  because well this really is only just  the beginning there are still so many  features that we need to add for example  as i mentioned blending between  animations is really really important we  also want inverse kinematics the  animation system also has to play nice  with the physics system these are the  kinds of things that the hazel team is  currently working on xerox has already  started working on the animation graph  system which will be really important  for deciding what states to blend  between and how and peter  peter's just having fun with ragdolls
 

